00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.020
So, today, we're going to
talk about dedicated game

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.840
servers for large-scale,
multiplayer games.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.390
If you're not familiar with
dedicated game servers, very

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:25.300
quickly, what they are
is a full simulation

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.100
of what's happening
inside a game,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.997
usually running
somewhere in the cloud.

00:00:28.997 --> 00:00:30.580
And what happens is,
is the people who

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.320
are playing a
particular game session

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.960
will all connect to an
individual dedicated game

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.760
server process.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.700
Usually they'll send things
like, hey, I'm moving forward

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.000
or maybe I did an action
like fired a rocket.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.005
And it's up to that
dedicated game server

00:00:45.005 --> 00:00:46.630
to basically be the
authority of what's

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.810
happening inside that game
and send that information back

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.240
to the players so that they
know exactly what's happening

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.160
inside the game.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.550
This is really important for
large-scale, multiplayer games

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.230
because it enables
people to have

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.960
a lot of control over
the latency experience

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.640
that people have with
these real-time games.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.880
This is important because
you have geographic control

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.850
over exactly where your
dedicated game servers exist

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.550
because players around the world
are going to play your game.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.490
So if you have very low
latency requirements, which

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.460
many real-time,
multiplayer games do,

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.310
you can put it in a particular
place around the world

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:27.000
so that you can have control and
have knowledge over exactly how

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.740
fast the information
is going backwards

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.450
and forwards from these
dedicated game servers.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.300
So, that sounds simple
in practice, right?

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.130
But orchestrating
these thousands

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.780
of machines and
potentially thousands

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.270
of dedicated game server
processes around the world

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.170
is essentially a tricky problem.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.500
There's a lot of moving parts
that you need to look at

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.025
and a lot of things that
could potentially go wrong.

00:01:48.025 --> 00:01:50.400
So that's really what I want
to talk to you about today--

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.150
how are we solving that
problem with Google Cloud Game

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.700
Servers and the Agones project.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.950
So let's start from the
foundation, some things you've

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.470
probably already heard of.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.330
So many of you are
probably already

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.340
familiar with the
Kubernetes project.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.290
If you're not, it's
an open-source project

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.150
initially started
by Google that's

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.880
built for running
processes or software

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.730
processes over a large number
of machines inside a cluster.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.350
Kubernetes is fantastic.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.440
You can come to Google Cloud,
run it on our GKE platform.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.450
You can also run it wherever
you want to run it, as well.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.020
This is super useful
for game servers

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.790
because it means,
